import ./checker_scalar_locals.vibe {
  scalar_local_sites_mark, scalar_local_sites_rewind
}
// lib/@vibe/compiler/checker -- a region's token kept by code written outside
// the region body (#3343).

//# #3276 checks a `TaskGroup::run` body written as a closure literal inside
//# the group's region: a write the body makes, or a closure written in it
//# makes, is refused when it stores the group's token in a location declared
//# before the body. Code written ANYWHERE ELSE was checked where it was
//# written, outside every region, so it kept the token unseen and the handle
//# was joined after its group had closed:
//#
//#   - a helper that stores its argument
//#     (`fn put[T](c: Cell[T], v: T) { c.v = Some(v) }`, called as
//#     `put(c, handle)`), which ADR-0090's `region r { .. }` shared;
//#   - a closure bound before the call (`let stash = (h) -> { c.v = Some(h) }`
//#     called as `stash(handle)`);
//#   - a body passed by name (`TaskGroup::run(body)`).
//#
//# The checker can see those bodies, so it checks each one again where the
//# token reaches it. A call made inside an active region that hands a value
//# carrying the region to a function declared in this module, or to a
//# `let`-bound closure literal, re-checks the callee's lambda with:
//#
//#   - the call's regions bound above the callee's own scope (the module for a
//#     top-level function, the defining scope for a closure), so a top-level
//#     binding and every binding the callee captured are outside them, as they
//#     would be for a write written in the body;
//#   - each parameter bound to its argument's type at this call, so a value
//#     derived from the token carries the region even where the callee's
//#     annotation spells its type without one (`TaskHandle[Int]`);
//#   - each parameter whose argument is a location declared before one of
//#     those regions marked as an alias of an outer binding for exactly those
//#     regions, so a write into it is judged as a write into the argument;
//#   - each parameter whose argument is a function marked with what that
//#     function is, so a call of it inside the callee is judged in turn.
//#
//# The ordinary write checks then answer, and a write they refuse refuses the
//# call, with the call's own diagnostic. What the callee RETURNS is not judged
//# at the call: the re-check also finds which regions the callee's result
//# carries, and the call's result carries them into the caller (see
//# "Provenance a type does not show" below), so the result of `start(g)`
//# declared `-> TaskHandle[Int]`, or of a helper returning a struct built from
//# the handle, is judged where the caller writes, returns or passes it on --
//# and a caller that joins it inside the body is legal. A closure is
//# re-checked when it
//# captured a value carrying the region, too, since it may hand that value to
//# a function it is passed. The re-check is a throwaway lane: its diagnostics,
//# type table and substitution are dropped, its fresh variables come from a
//# range the real check never reaches, and the per-run tables it touches are
//# restored afterwards.
//#
//# A function VALUE with no visible body is judged by where it comes from. A
//# parameter of a lambda written inside the region (or re-checked under it) is
//# answered for at that lambda's own calls, where its argument is known. Any
//# other -- a parameter of the function that minted the region, a value a call
//# returned, a `let mut` closure, a function stored in a field -- is refused
//# when it is handed the region, since nothing about what it keeps is known. A
//# function imported from another module is judged by the summary of what it
//# keeps that its module published (`checker_region_retention_summary.vibe`,
//# #3360); one that published none keeps nothing.

